-- title:
--   Lifting Theta_f-values along square-zero surjections
-- statement:
--   The statement is set up with the following data and groups of hypotheses.
--
--   **Numerical data.** Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, and a nonzero natural number $N$ with $r \nmid N$, $\bar r \nmid N$ and $N$ squarefree.
--
--   **Base rings (`hdvr`–`hvdet`).** A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring, an irreducible element $\pi$, $\mathcal O$ complete for the $\pi$-adic topology, with residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ of cardinality $r$ and $(r) = (\pi)$; a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$. Further, a characteristic-zero domain $Onr$ over $\mathcal O$ with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $Fr$, such that $Onr$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, that ideal is maximal, every element of $Onr$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$, every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$, and $Fr(x) \equiv x^r \pmod{\pi}$; thus $Onr$ plays the role of the completion of the maximal unramified extension and $Fr$ of the Frobenius lift. Finally a homomorphism $\mathrm{vdet} : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by `hvdet`: $\mathrm{vdet}(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\pi^n$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Quaternionic moduli data.** Rationals $a,b$ with `hB`: $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$ the algebra $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b] \otimes \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ contains $r$ or $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda$. A scheme $\mathcal X$ with $f : \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and an assignment `pt` of $\mathcal X$-points over $s$ to fake elliptic curves of level $N$, satisfying `h𝒳` : `IsCoarseModuli` (isomorphism-invariance, compatibility with pullback, bijectivity on points over algebraically closed fields, and the universal property). A level $n \ge 3$ with $r \nmid n$, $\bar r \nmid n$, $n$ coprime to $N$; a scheme $M$ with $fM : M \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and `ptF` on curves with full level-$n$ structure, satisfying `hM` : `IsFineModuli` (isomorphism-invariance, pullback compatibility, and bijectivity of `ptF` on $S$-points for all $S$); a group $G$ acting by $\rho : G \to \operatorname{Aut} M$ with labels $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ and `hρ` : `IsLevelTwistAction` (the automorphisms lie over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, $\rho(g)$ realises the level twist by $\chi(g)$, and the labels are unital, multiplicative, surjective and injective modulo $n$). A morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ with $p$ followed by $f$ equal to $fM$, invariant under every $\rho(h)$, and compatible with the point maps (`hp_pt`). For every prime $\ell \neq r,\bar r$, a coarse moduli scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ with structure morphism $g_\ell$ and point map `ptT` for curves with extra level $\ell$ (`h𝒴`), together with two degeneracy maps $d_0\ell, d_1\ell : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over $f$ that on points forget the level-$\ell$ subgroup, respectively pass to the quotient by a level-$\ell$ isogeny (`hd₀`, `hd₁`). Atkin–Lehner morphisms $ar, arbar : \mathcal X \to \mathcal X$ over $f$ which on points carry a curve to its Atkin–Lehner quotient at $r$, respectively at $\bar r$.
--
--   **The definite side.** Rationals $a_1, b_1$ with `hdef`: $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$ and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ ramified exactly at $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda_1$, an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ contained in $\Lambda_1$; a finite-adelic unit $n_1$ in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ such that $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1$ is Eichler of level $Nr$; an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra embedding $\iota_0$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ into $M_2(K_0)$; a height-one prime $v$ containing $r$. A subgroup $\Gamma t$ of the units, equal to the group of units integral away from $v$; elements $s_\ell$ and finite-adelic units $sf_\ell$ for each prime $\ell \neq r,\bar r$ subject to `hs` (four clauses: $sf_\ell$ has component $s_\ell \otimes 1$ at every prime not containing $r$, component $1$ at primes containing $r$, the product of the diagonal image of the scalar $\ell$ with $sf_\ell^{-1}$ lies in the level Hecke set of $\Lambda_1$ and $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1$ at $\ell$ when $\ell \mid N$ and in the prime Hecke set of $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1$ at $\ell$ otherwise, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell) = \ell$); and $\Gamma t_\ell = \Gamma t \cap s_\ell \Gamma t s_\ell^{-1}$. A unit $\bar w$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\Gamma t$.
--
--   **Special-fibre data (`hΛℤ`–`hE5e`).** The hypothesis that every rational integer lies in $\Lambda$, and a coordinate map $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to \mathbb Z_{r^2}^{\,2}$ (values in pairs of Witt vectors over $\mathbb F_{r^2}$) which is additive, unital, multiplicative for the Frobenius-twisted product rule, injective, dense, and compatible with traces. A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ of level $N$ over $Onr/(\pi)$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ of height $4$ over that ring, formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of dimension $2$ along $A_0$, with `hA₀` identifying the formal group of $A_0$ with $X_0$ compatibly with $\mathrm{coord}$; endomorphisms $e\gamma, e'\gamma$ of $A_0$ over the base for $\gamma \in \Gamma t$ and degrees $\deg \gamma$. The clauses `hE1`, `hE1mul`, `hE1sc`, `hE1'`, `hE2`, `hE3`, `hE4` (summarised here) state: each pair $(e\gamma, e'\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg\gamma}$ preserving the level structure; $e$ is multiplicative up to multiplication by powers of $r$; scalars $\gamma = c$ act as multiplication by $c$ up to powers of $r$, and conversely only scalars act, up to powers of $r$, by a positive integer; for every injective embedding of the centraliser of the $X_0$-action and of $X_0.\mathrm{varpiEnd}$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is a single $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that each $e\gamma$ is induced on formal coordinates by a centraliser element whose image is $r^{k_\gamma} g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$; each full level-$n$ structure on $A_0$ carries $\Gamma t$-labels in $\Lambda$, multiplicative and scalar-compatible modulo $n$; and every level-preserving isogeny of $r$-power degree of a curve pulled back from $A_0$ over an algebraically closed field comes from some $\gamma \in \Gamma t$ up to powers of $r$. An order $R_2 \le R_1$ with $r$-power denominators sufficing to carry $R_1$ into $R_2$, together with $\hat e : R_2 \to \mathrm{End}(A_0)$ over the base and the clauses `hE5a`–`hE5e` (summarised here): $\hat e$ is additive on points, commutes with the $\Lambda$-action and preserves the level; it is unital, multiplicative and agrees with the $\Lambda$-action on integers; $\hat e(\bar x)\circ \hat e(x)$ is multiplication by $\mathrm{nrd}(x)$; it agrees with $e$ on $r$-power multiples of elements of $\Gamma t$; and $\Gamma t$ and $R_2$ are simultaneously linearised into $M_2(K_0)$ by one $g$ as above.
--
--   **Formal moduli and the Drinfeld comparison (`ι`–`hPiexD`).** A ring homomorphism $\iota : \mathbb Z_{r^2} \to Onr$; a special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ of height $4$ over $Onr/(r)$ relative to $\iota$; a moduli package $MD$ over $Onr$ which is a Zariski sheaf; a map $\eta$ sending rigidified objects to $MD$-points, with `hη` (three clauses: on admissible rigidifications $\eta$ identifies points exactly for isomorphic rigidifications, $\eta$ is natural in the base ring, and every $MD$-point is, locally for a finite cover of the base by principal opens, of the form $\eta(t)$ for an admissible $t$); an injective homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $\Phi$-action and $\Phi.\mathrm{varpiEnd}$ into $M_2(K_0)$ whose image is commensurable with $M_2(\mathcal O)$ in the precise sense of `hE₀` (a single exponent $m$ with $r^m M_2(\mathcal O)$ inside the image and $r^m$ times the image inside $M_2(\mathcal O)$). A family of maps $eD$ from the points of the functor $\mathcal G(\mathcal O, MD)$ to pairs consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $Onr \to B$ and a Deligne datum for $(K_0,\pi)$ over $B$, defined for Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebras $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, which is natural (`hnatD`), bijective (`hbijD`), compatible with the unramified leg (`hfstD`), turns the action of $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ on $\mathcal G$-points into the twisted action on pairs (`hGLD`), turns $\pi$-translation into the Frobenius twist by $1$ on the leg leaving the datum unchanged (`hPiD`), and for which $\pi$-translates always exist (`hPiexD`). A ring map $\kappa : Onr/(\pi) \to Onr/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps, and an isogeny $\beta_0$ of height $4 n_0$ from $\Phi$ to the reduction of $X_0$ along $\kappa$.
--
--   **Atkin–Lehner datum at $\bar r$ in the special fibre.** A full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$; a curve $A_{0w}$ with morphisms $aw, aw'$ exhibiting it as the Atkin–Lehner quotient of $A_0$ at $\bar r$ (`hALw`), morphisms $bw, bw'$ forming an isogeny pair of degree $r^{k_w}$ (`hBSw`) with $bw$ level-preserving (`hBSwlev`), and `hJOINTw`, which for every injective embedding of the centraliser provides one $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ linearising simultaneously the $\Gamma t$-action as $r^{k_\gamma} g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$ and the composite $aw$ followed by $bw$ as $r^{k_w} g\,\iota_0(\bar w)\,g^{-1}$.
--
--   **The comparison $\Xi$ (`hΞleg`–`hΞdef`).** A map $\Xi$ sending, for each Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and each $\psi : Onr \to B$, a curve rigidified along $A_0$ to a $\mathcal G$-point of $MD$, subject to: `hΞleg`, for $B$ with no idempotents other than $0$ and $1$ the leg of $\Xi$ is a Frobenius twist $Fr^k \circ \psi$; `hΞnat`, compatibility with base change along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps and pullbacks of rigidifications; `hΞiso`, invariance under isomorphisms of rigidified curves admitting the explicit comparison data (a map of the reductions, a pullback of the auxiliary curves, and agreement of the isogenies up to powers of $r$); and `hΞdef`, for connected $B$ with $r$ nilpotent, the explicit description of $\Xi$ as $\eta$ applied to a rigidification $t$ with $t.X$ the given formal module, obtained by rigidity transport relative to $\theta_0, \kappa, \beta_0$ at some $j \le 1$, admissible for the leg twisted by $Fr^{-j}$, with leg $Fr^{-j}\circ\psi$.
--
--   **Equivariance and uniformisation (`g₀`, `heqΓ`–`hC0u`).** An element $g_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that, for connected $B$: `heqΓ`, if two rigidifications of a curve differ by translation by $e\gamma$ then their $\Xi$-images are related by the action of $r^c\,g_0\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g_0^{-1}$ for some $c \in \mathbb Z$; `heqW`, if $\bar r$ is invertible in $B$ and a curve admits an Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$, then rigidifications related by the comparison data built from $aw$ followed by $bw$ have $\Xi$-images related by the action of $r^c\,g_0\,\iota_0(\bar w)\,g_0^{-1}$. The four uniformisation clauses are: `hK1`, for connected $B$, every leg $\psi$ and every Deligne datum $P$ arise from a curve $E$ with a rigidification $\varrho$ relative to the leg twisted by $-\mathrm{vdet}(g_0)$, whose $\Xi$-image has that leg and whose $eD$-image is carried to $(\psi,P)$ by the $g_0^{-1}$-twisted action; `hK2`, any two such pairs are isomorphic, by an isomorphism of curves together with the explicit comparison of their rigidifications; `hK3`, such pairs base change along any $\mathcal O$-algebra map, compatibly with the transport of $P$; `hK4`, such pairs lift along a surjection with square-zero kernel, pinning the leg and matching the twisted-action condition for all Deligne data downstairs. Finally `hC0e` and `hC0u`: for connected $B$ the height-normalised level-$n$ transport of $P_0$ along a rigidification exists, and its underlying section is unique.
--
--   **The map $\Theta_f$.** For each Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, a map $\Theta_f$ from triples consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : Onr \to B$, a Deligne datum $P$ over $B$, and an element $g \in G$, to the set of morphisms $\operatorname{Spec} B \to M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$; it is natural in $B$ (`hΘnat`); its value is computed by `hΘval`: whenever $(E,\varrho)$ has pinned leg and $eD$-image carried to $(\psi,P)$ by the $g_0^{-1}$-twisted action and $P_n$ is a height-normalised level transport of $P_0$, then $\Theta_f((\psi,P),g)$ is the point $\mathrm{ptF}$ of $(E,P_n)$ followed by the automorphism $\rho(g^{-1})$; and by `hΘvalc`: that point followed by $p$ is the coarse point $\mathrm{pt}$ of $E$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For all Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebras $B$ and $B_0$, every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $p : B \to B_0$, and nilpotency of the images of $\pi$ in $B$ and in $B_0$, assume that $p$ is surjective and that the kernel of $p$ is square-zero in the pointwise form: $st = 0$ whenever $p(s) = p(t) = 0$. Then for every triple $x_0$ consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $Onr \to B_0$, a Deligne datum over $B_0$ and an element of $G$, and every morphism $y : \operatorname{Spec} B \to M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ whose image under the functorial map along $p$ equals $\Theta_f$ of $x_0$, there exists a triple $x$ over $B$ such that the functorial image of $x$ along $p$ equals $x_0$, and $\Theta_f$ of $x$ equals $y$.
--
--   No connectedness assumption on $B$ or $B_0$ occurs in the conclusion; the conditions on idempotents occur only inside the hypotheses `hΞleg`, `hΞdef`, `heqΓ`, `heqW`, `hK1`, `hK2` and `hC0e`.
--
--   This is the infinitesimal surjectivity (lifting) half of the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison on nilpotent points: it says that the uniformisation map $\Theta_f$ from the functor of unramified legs, Deligne data on the formal upper half-plane and level twists to the nilpotent points of the fine moduli scheme $M$ lifts values along surjections with square-zero kernel. It is used, together with the corresponding uniqueness statement, by [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_fineFamily_of_evenRigidifiedPair_of_isNoetherianRing_heightNormalised_conn`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_fineFamily_of_evenRigidifiedPair_of_isNoetherianRing_heightNormalised_conn); the proof combines the local lifting result `fineFamily_exists_lift_locally_of_value_of_squareZero`, the uniqueness statement `fineFamily_lift_unique_of_value_of_squareZero_heightNormalised_conn`, the Zariski-descent and separatedness of Deligne data, and the unique lifting of the unramified leg along square-zero surjections.
